WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.778 --> 00:00:21.923
Mr. Rochester had given me only a week's leave of absence, yet a month passed before I departed

2
00:00:21.983 --> 00:00:22.684
from Gateshead.

3
00:00:23.865 --> 00:00:31.411
Eliza and Georgiana needed my assistance—with sewing and packing and other household tasks—in order to begin their new

4
00:00:31.471 --> 00:00:37.520
lives. As I will have no occasion to speak of my cousins again, I might as well mention

5
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.064
here that Georgiana eventually married a wealthy, older man in London.

6
00:00:43.285 --> 00:00:49.030
Eliza traveled to France, where she became a nun and is now the mother superior of a convent.

7
00:00:50.352 --> 00:00:52.173
While I was away, I heard from Mrs.

8
00:00:52.213 --> 00:00:56.817
Fairfax. She wrote that the houseguests had dispersed and Mr.

9
00:00:56.877 --> 00:00:59.340
Rochester had left for London three weeks ago.

10
00:01:00.552 --> 00:01:06.176
Mrs. Fairfax surmised that he had gone to make arrangements for his wedding because he was talking about

11
00:01:06.216 --> 00:01:07.557
purchasing a new carriage.

12
00:01:08.798 --> 00:01:14.522
From what everybody had said, and from what she had seen, she believed that the event would shortly

13
00:01:14.602 --> 00:01:15.222
take place.

14
00:01:17.164 --> 00:01:21.827
But the idea of his marrying Blanche Ingram still seemed strange to her.

15
00:01:23.048 --> 00:01:26.610
"I have no doubt that he will marry Miss Ingram," I said to myself.

16
00:01:27.891 --> 00:01:30.741
My question was, where would I go when he did?

17
00:01:30.761 --> 00:01:34.084
I dreamed of Miss Ingram all that night.

18
00:01:35.305 --> 00:01:41.510
In one particularly vivid dream, I saw her closing the gates of Thornfield against me and pointing down

19
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:42.031
the road.

20
00:01:43.292 --> 00:01:48.656
Mr. Rochester looked on with his arms folded and smiled scornfully at both of us.

21
00:01:49.857 --> 00:01:52.599
My journey back to Thornfield seemed very tedious.

22
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.662
Once there, how long would I stay?

23
00:01:56.903 --> 00:01:58.124
Not long, I was sure.

24
00:01:59.336 --> 00:02:00.557
I had not notified Mrs.

25
00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:05.480
Fairfax of the exact date of my arrival because I didn't want a carriage to meet me at

26
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:11.704
Millcote. After leaving my trunk at the inn to be sent on later, I took the old road

27
00:02:11.744 --> 00:02:12.524
to Thornfield.

28
00:02:13.765 --> 00:02:19.709
It was a little-used road, running mainly through fields, where the haymakers were at work on this mild

29
00:02:19.789 --> 00:02:20.509
June evening.

30
00:02:22.451 --> 00:02:24.192
As I walked along, I felt glad.

31
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:31.004
In fact, I was so happy that I stopped once to ask myself what this joy meant and

32
00:02:31.024 --> 00:02:34.166
to remind myself that I was not returning to my home.

33
00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:36.288
"To be sure, Mrs.

34
00:02:36.348 --> 00:02:39.650
Fairfax will smile and welcome you," I said to myself.

35
00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:44.193
"And little Adele will clap her hands and jump up to greet you.

36
00:02:45.394 --> 00:02:49.677
But you know very well that you are thinking of someone else and that he is not thinking

37
00:02:49.777 --> 00:02:50.198
of you."

38
00:02:51.579 --> 00:02:55.458
Still, for me, it was enough just to see Mr.

39
00:02:55.498 --> 00:02:56.399
Rochester again.

40
00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:02.464
And so I ran to be with him while I could before I was parted from him forever.

41
00:03:03.645 --> 00:03:07.508
I had only a field or two to cross, and then I would reach the gates of Thornfield.

42
00:03:08.649 --> 00:03:10.911
How full the hedges were of roses!

43
00:03:11.612 --> 00:03:15.495
But I had no time to gather any flowers because I wanted to be at the house.

44
00:03:16.716 --> 00:03:20.700
I passed a tall hedge, shooting flowering branches across the path.

45
00:03:22.556 --> 00:03:27.219
I saw a narrow stile with stone steps, and I saw—Mr.

46
00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:32.522
Rochester! He was sitting there, a book and pencil in his hand, writing.

47
00:03:33.783 --> 00:03:35.684
Well, he was not a ghost.

48
00:03:36.844 --> 00:03:40.867
But all my nerves were unstrung, and I had to fight to master my emotions.

49
00:03:42.007 --> 00:03:42.848
What did this mean?

50
00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:48.431
I did not think I would tremble like this when I saw him or lose my voice or

51
00:03:48.491 --> 00:03:49.452
ability to move.

52
00:03:50.684 --> 00:03:54.825
There was no need to make an absolute fool of myself; I could go another way to the

53
00:03:54.865 --> 00:03:57.446
house. But it was too late.

54
00:03:58.566 --> 00:03:59.346
He had seen me.

55
00:03:59.366 --> 00:04:03.268
"There you are, appearing out of nowhere like a fairy!

56
00:04:04.388 --> 00:04:05.128
Welcome home!"

57
00:04:05.488 --> 00:04:07.689
he cried as he put down his book and pencil.

58
00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:12.030
"Absent from me a whole month and forgetting me too, I'll swear."

59
00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:18.472
I knew there would be pleasure in meeting my master again even though he would soon cease being

60
00:04:18.532 --> 00:04:19.132
my master.

61
00:04:20.308 --> 00:04:23.989
His words seemed to imply that it mattered to him whether I forgot him or not.

62
00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:28.090
And he spoke of Thornfield as my home.

63
00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.731
If only it were my home!

64
00:04:31.991 --> 00:04:33.311
I struggled to appear calm.

65
00:04:33.331 --> 00:04:36.252
"Did you have a good trip to London, sir?"

66
00:04:36.272 --> 00:04:39.353
"Yes, how did you know about that?"

67
00:04:39.373 --> 00:04:42.314
"Mrs. Fairfax told me in a letter."

68
00:04:42.334 --> 00:04:45.595
"And did she tell you what I went there to do?"

69
00:04:45.615 --> 00:04:47.575
"Oh yes, sir!

70
00:04:48.816 --> 00:04:50.076
Everybody knew your errand."

71
00:04:51.188 --> 00:04:54.891
"You must see the new carriage, Jane, and tell me how you think it will suit Mrs.

72
00:04:54.971 --> 00:04:58.974
Rochester. Don't you think she'll look like a queen riding in it?

73
00:05:00.175 --> 00:05:02.336
I wish I were as handsome as she is beautiful.

74
00:05:03.557 --> 00:05:07.079
Fairy that you are, can't you give me a charm to make me a handsome man?"

75
00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:11.523
To him I said, "That's beyond the power of magic."

76
00:05:13.464 --> 00:05:18.908
But to myself I added, "A loving eye is all that's needed to see that you're handsome enough."

77
00:05:20.168 --> 00:05:23.090
Mr. Rochester gave me a smile that he rarely used.

78
00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:29.093
He seemed to think it too good for everyday purposes, but he shed it over me now like

79
00:05:29.253 --> 00:05:35.076
sunshine. "Pass, Jane," he said, making room for me to cross the stile.

80
00:05:35.096 --> 00:05:39.218
"Go home and rest your traveling feet at a friend's doorway."

81
00:05:40.359 --> 00:05:45.282
All I had to do was to obey him in silence, but after I crossed the stile, a

82
00:05:45.422 --> 00:05:46.922
force turned me around.

83
00:05:46.942 --> 00:05:48.823
"Thank you, Mr.

84
00:05:48.883 --> 00:05:51.193
Rochester, for your great kindness.

85
00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:54.855
I am strangely glad to get back again to you.

86
00:05:55.916 --> 00:05:58.717
Wherever you are is my home—my only home."

87
00:05:59.938 --> 00:06:03.921
I walked on so fast that even he couldn't have overtaken me if he'd tried.

88
00:06:05.782 --> 00:06:08.163
Two weeks of dubious calm followed my return.

89
00:06:09.424 --> 00:06:10.505
Nothing was said of Mr.

90
00:06:10.565 --> 00:06:15.708
Rochester's impending marriage, and I saw no preparations going on for such an event.

91
00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:18.449
Almost every day I asked Mrs.

92
00:06:18.489 --> 00:06:23.170
Fairfax if she had heard of any plans, but her answer was always in the negative.

93
00:06:24.631 --> 00:06:29.792
Once she'd actually asked him when he was going to bring home his bride, and he'd answered her

94
00:06:29.932 --> 00:06:32.033
only with a joke and an odd look.

95
00:06:33.333 --> 00:06:38.915
One thing especially surprised me: there were no visits back and forth between Mr.

96
00:06:38.975 --> 00:06:40.616
Rochester and Blanche Ingram.

97
00:06:41.748 --> 00:06:46.949
To be sure, Ingram Park was twenty miles away, but that was only a morning's ride for a

98
00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:48.070
horseman such as Mr.

99
00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:55.112
Rochester. I began to cherish hopes that the match had been broken off, that one or both parties

100
00:06:55.192 --> 00:06:56.512
had changed their minds.

101
00:06:57.892 --> 00:06:59.913
Meanwhile never had Mr.

102
00:06:59.973 --> 00:07:02.834
Rochester called me more frequently to his presence.

103
00:07:04.154 --> 00:07:10.656
Never had he been kinder to me, and—alas, dear reader!—never had I loved him so well.
